  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the global development of hydrogen strategies, trade roles, and key milestones over time. A world map highlights countries by whether they are positioned as hydrogen exporters or importers and by the apparent source mix of planned hydrogen production. Visible categories distinguish mainly renewable-based hydrogen, renewable plus other sources, and pathways involving fossil fuels with carbon capture. Broadly, many countries in Europe and East Asia appear as import-oriented, while several countries with strong renewable resources or fossil capacity appear as potential exporters.\n\nNext to the map, a compact chart shows when national hydrogen strategies were introduced. It suggests a sharp acceleration after 2020, with many countries releasing plans in 2021–2024 compared with only a few early adopters in 2017–2019.\n\nThe lower timeline places this policy growth in a longer historical context, from early fuel-cell vehicle research and the “hydrogen economy” idea to commercial vehicles, refuelling stations, power-to-gas projects, and recent investment in clean hydrogen and ammonia. Milestones such as the Kyoto Protocol and Paris Agreement are included, linking hydrogen’s rise to climate policy. Overall, the figure conveys hydrogen’s transition from niche technology to a rapidly expanding global energy strategy.",
